WEBVTT

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:14.170
<v Ira Glass>When Rosie was a kid, they had a dog named Fu, a Shih Tzu. And Rosie's mom swears that she knows the exact day the dog went bad. It came when she heard about a discount way to get a dog grooming. It was this grooming school.
<v Ira Glass>罗茜小时候养过一只叫“福”的狗，是一只西施犬。罗茜的妈妈发誓，她清楚地记得那只狗变坏的确切日子。那天她听说有一种打折的宠物美容服务，就是那家美容学校。

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:25.950
<v Rosie Schaap>Her friend said that it didn't cost much, and they were very good. But my mother believes that this experience sort of ruined Fu. That somehow they tortured him and did something wrong in his first grooming.
<v Rosie Schaap>她的朋友说费用不高，而且他们做得很好。但我母亲认为那次经历毁了“福”。不知怎的，他们在第一次美容时虐待了它，或者做了什么错事。

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.980
<v Ira Glass>What? He went to get his hair cut, basically, and they ruined him as a dog?
<v Ira Glass>什么？它只不过是去剪个毛，结果他们毁了一只狗？

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:38.920
<v Rosie Schaap>Well, he got like shaved. And he got like full-on, summer shave. And he came back a different dog, according to my mother.
<v Rosie Schaap>嗯，它被剃了毛。就像那种彻底的、夏日款式的剃毛。据我母亲说，它回来时已经变成另一只狗了。

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:53.220
<v Ira Glass>After the grooming, he became a biter. Rosie got bit. Other people got bit. There were trips to the hospital, lots of drama. Fu hated people. And then the parrot arrived, Judy the parrot.
<v Ira Glass>美容之后，它开始咬人。罗茜被咬了。其他人也被咬了。经常要去医院，闹剧不断。“福”讨厌人类。然后鹦鹉来了，朱迪，那只鹦鹉。

00:00:53.220 --> 00:01:04.830
<v Ira Glass>The neighbors had actually bought this parrot without understanding what a lot of trouble that parrots can be. Parrots can be very anxious birds, especially when you leave them alone. They can pull out their feathers. They can screech like a smoke alarm.
<v Ira Glass>邻居们买这只鹦鹉时根本没意识到鹦鹉会带来多大的麻烦。鹦鹉是非常焦虑的鸟，尤其是当你把它们单独留下时。它们会拔掉自己的羽毛。它们会像火灾报警器一样尖叫。

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:30.730
<v Rosie Schaap>So they took the bird cage and put a note on it that said, take this bird or we're going to cook it, and left the cage in front of our door. We were like two doors down the hall. So we took her in. And we didn't know-- we'd had dogs and cats, but we'd never had a bird. And before you knew it, we all loved her. You know, my mother she'd let the bird perch on the edge of her coffee cup in the morning and drink her morning coffee with her.
<v Rosie Schaap>于是他们把鸟笼放在我们门口，上面贴了张纸条写着：“要么带走这只鸟，要么我们就要把它炖了”。我们就住在走廊尽头的两户人家，所以我们收留了它。我们不知道——我们养过猫狗，但从未养过鸟。没过多久，我们都爱上了它。你知道，我母亲早上会让鸟儿停在她咖啡杯的边缘，陪她一起喝早咖啡。

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:49.490
<v Rosie Schaap>And Fu and Judy just quickly became best friends. I don't know what it was, but they just connected. And it was very cute, because you'd see this little, matted Shih Tzu and this like eight inch high parrot pacing the floor of the apartment together like they were walking down the hall having a chat.
<v Rosie Schaap>“福”和朱迪很快就成了最好的朋友。我不知道是什么原因，但它们就是产生了共鸣。那场面非常可爱，因为你会看到这只毛发打结的小西施犬和这只八英寸高的鹦鹉一起在公寓的地板上踱步，就像它们在走廊里聊天散步一样。

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:53.870
<v Ira Glass>Dog and parrot kind of strolling.
<v Ira Glass>狗和鹦鹉在悠闲地散步。

00:01:53.870 --> 00:02:12.040
<v Rosie Schaap>They would stroll together. And Fu would roll on his back. He hated it when any person came near his stomach. She could crawl all over him. And she did. She would crawl up his chest, up to his face, peck at his face like she was giving him kisses.
<v Rosie Schaap>它们会一起散步。“福”还会翻身露出肚皮。它讨厌任何人靠近它的肚子。但朱迪可以在它身上爬来爬去。它确实也这么做了。它会爬上它的胸口，爬到它的脸上，啄它的脸，就像在亲吻它一样。

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.120
<v Ira Glass>This dog that would actually try to bite all of you?
<v Ira Glass>这只曾经试图咬你们所有人的狗？

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:28.480
<v Rosie Schaap>Yes, he loved it, loved the parrot. I mean, he'd had dog girlfriends before, and he got along fine with the cats. But we'd never seen him develop a friendship like that.
<v Rosie Schaap>是的，它很喜欢，很爱这只鹦鹉。我是说，它以前也有过狗女友，和猫相处得也很好。但我们从未见过它建立过那样的友谊。

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.190
<v Ira Glass>Oh, this is like the big--
<v Ira Glass>噢，这就是那伟大的——

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:33.890
<v Rosie Schaap>This was the big friendship, big romance.
<v Rosie Schaap>这就是那段伟大的友谊，伟大的浪漫。

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.320
<v Ira Glass>Of his life?
<v Ira Glass>它一生的？

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:41.140
<v Rosie  Schaap>I think so.
<v Rosie  Schaap>我想是吧。

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:50.950
<v Ira  Glass>So this went on for a long, long time. And then, one day, Fu came into the kitchen to eat, and the whole family was there including the parrot, Judy.
<v Ira  Glass>这种情况持续了很长很长一段时间。然后有一天，“福”走进厨房准备吃东西，全家人都在那里，包括鹦鹉朱迪。

00:02:50.950 --> 00:03:22.450
<v Rosie Schaap>And she flew down to the floor and went over to Fu's bowls and, as I remember it, drank a little bit of his water. But then, she went into his food dish. And I don't know how that hadn't happened before. She took some bit of dog food into her beak, and, in just like a split second, he went over and bit the top-feathers right out of her head.
<v Rosie Schaap>它飞到地板上，走到“福”的碗边，我记得它喝了一点水。但随后，它伸进它的食盆。我不知道之前怎么没发生过这种情况。它用喙叼走了一些狗粮，就在那一瞬间，它跑过去，把她头顶上的毛给咬秃了。

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:35.010
<v Rosie Schaap>And she squawked, and squawked, and squawked. And sort of hyperventilated in this very alarming bird way, because they're so tiny, and you can hear them panting, and it sounds like they're going to just die from anxiety.
<v Rosie Schaap>她尖叫着，尖叫着，尖叫着。以一种非常令人不安的鸟类方式急促呼吸，因为它们太小了，你能听到它们喘气，听起来好像它们就要因为焦虑而死了一样。

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:43.330
<v Ira Glass>It's interesting how the traumatic event in his life was a bad haircut, and then he, basically, turns around and gives that to her.
<v Ira Glass>有趣的是，它生命中的创伤性事件是一次糟糕的理发，然后它转头就把同样的遭遇给了她。

00:03:43.330 --> 00:04:21.590
<v Rosie Schaap>Yeah, but the bird was just ready to pass-out, she was so terrified. But after that, she pretty much stayed in my room with the door closed. Fu would come to the bedroom door and scratch, and scratch, and cry, and cry. And it was just awful. And she would usually squawk, not in a friendly, hey buddy, I miss you way, but in a fearful way. It was his best friend, and he'd just sit there at the door sort of scratching and pining. And that was that, really.
<v Rosie Schaap>是啊，但这只鸟当时快晕过去了，它太害怕了。在那之后，它基本就待在我的房间里，关着门。“福”会来到卧室门口抓门，不停地抓、不停地哭。真的太可怕了。它通常会发出尖叫，不是那种友好的、“嘿伙计，我想你”的叫声，而是一种恐惧的叫声。那是它最好的朋友，而它就坐在门边，抓着门，渴望着。事情就是这样，真的。

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:31.010
<v Ira  Glass>So he drove his best friend away.
<v Ira  Glass>所以它赶走了它最好的朋友。

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:41.720
<v Rosie Schaap>Because he just snapped. In one moment he was like a dog more than he was a parrot's pal.
<v Rosie Schaap>因为它突然失控了。在那一刻，它更多是一只狗，而不是鹦鹉的朋友。

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:54.330
<v Ira Glass>It was like suddenly he turned into a real dog, and then it's like she realized, oh, he's a dog. My friend is a dog. And then she became a parrot again.
<v Ira Glass>就像它突然变成了一只真正的狗，而它意识到，哦，它是一只狗。我的朋友是一只狗。然后她又变回了一只鹦鹉。

00:04:54.330 --> 00:05:10.670
<v Rosie  Schaap>I don't know if she realized he was a dog, and she was a bird. Whatever their essential animal nature's, what we knew was that they had been best friends. And in some ridiculous, anthropomorphic way, it looked very romantic. And then it was totally betrayed.
<v Rosie  Schaap>我不知道她是否意识到它是狗，她是鸟。无论它们基本的动物本性如何，我们所知道的是它们曾是最好的朋友。以某种荒谬的、拟人化的方式来看，这显得非常浪漫。然后却被彻底背叛了。

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:12.950
<v Ira  Glass>When he snapped at her.
<v Ira  Glass>当它对她发火时。

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:19.410
<v Rosie Schaap>When he snapped at her. And then it was over. I think it was just betrayal.
<v Rosie Schaap>当它对她发火时。然后一切就结束了。我认为这仅仅是背叛。

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:25.650
<v Ira Glass>I know, but he made one, little mistake. I don't know why, but I'm identifying with the dog here, very strongly.
<v Ira Glass>我知道，但它犯了一个小错误。我不知道为什么，但我现在非常强烈地认同这只狗。

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:39.850
<v Rosie Schaap>Well, we did too. We did too. It was horrible to hear him pining at the bedroom door. I mean, it really was like this awful, tragic romance. And he pined, and pined, and pined.
<v Rosie Schaap>嗯，我们也一样。我们也一样。听着它在卧室门口渴望的样子太可怕了。我是说，这真的就像一段糟糕、悲惨的恋情。它渴望着，渴望着，一直渴望着。

00:05:39.850 --> 00:06:20.000
<v Ira Glass>He pined for her, Rosie says, until the day he died. Judy never forgave him. We talked to anybody who knows about parrots, they'll tell you that people are constantly misunderstanding them and underestimating them. I was talking to this guy for this week's show who did research with parrots at MIT. And he was explaining to me how they're amazingly smart. For instance, he says that it's actually a myth that they only mimic words. They actually learn the words. They use them correctly. But it's one thing for people to misunderstand parrots, until I heard about Fu and Judy, I had no idea that other animals might make the same mistake. If only Fu knew how to treat a parrot.
<v Ira Glass>罗茜说，它一直渴望着她，直到死的那天。“朱迪”从未原谅它。我们和任何了解鹦鹉的人交谈，他们都会告诉你，人们总是误解和低估它们。我为本周的节目采访了一位在麻省理工学院研究鹦鹉的家伙。他向我解释了它们是多么聪明。例如，他说它们只会模仿词汇其实是个误区。它们实际上是在学习这些词。它们能正确地使用它们。但人们误解鹦鹉是一回事，在听说“福”和朱迪的故事之前，我不知道其他动物也会犯同样的错误。要是“福”知道该怎么对待鹦鹉就好了。

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:44.280
<v Ira Glass>But today on our show, we have stories of people underestimating what certain animals are like. Not just parrots, no, no, no, how narrow would that be. How small minded, how limited the vision and scope. No, no, we will not just confine ourselves to parrots this hour. We will also be talking about pot-bellied pigs. It's the Parrot and the Pot-bellied Pig. By the end of this hour, people get thrown in jail, relationships end, love affairs start, all because of these two animals.
<v Ira Glass>但在今天的节目中，我们要讲一些人们低估动物的故事。不只是鹦鹉，不，不，不，那太狭隘了。太鼠目寸光，视野和范围太局限了。不，不，这一小时我们不会局限于鹦鹉。我们还要谈谈大肚猪。这就是“鹦鹉与大肚猪”。这一小时结束时，会有人入狱、关系破裂、恋情开始，所有的一切都是因为这两只动物。

00:06:44.280 --> 00:07:07.940
<v Ira Glass>For WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Act one of our show today, Parrot. Act two-- Pig. Act three- Combo Platter, in which David Sedaris has a story with both the parrot and the pot-bellied pig. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是WBEZ芝加哥电台的《这就是美国生活》，由公共广播国际公司分发。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们今天节目的第一幕，鹦鹉。第二幕——猪。第三幕——大杂烩，大卫·塞达里斯将讲述一个同时涉及鹦鹉和大肚猪的故事。请继续收听。

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:35.160
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Parrot. Eric and Alex have been telling their parrot story since they were in school years ago. They've told this story many, many times to friends, to family, to strangers. In fact, the night before they came into the studio to tell this story, they went to a bar, and they practiced telling the story to random customers including, at one point, an actual movie star, who happen to be in town filming a big, blockbuster movie.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，鹦鹉。埃里克和亚历克斯自从多年前在学校起就一直讲述着他们的鹦鹉故事。他们已经把这个故事讲了非常非常多次，讲给朋友、家人、陌生人听。事实上，在他们走进录音室讲述这个故事的前一天晚上，他们去了一家酒吧，练习向随机的顾客讲这个故事，其中有一次，甚至包括一位碰巧在城里拍摄一部大制作电影的电影明星。

00:07:35.160 --> 00:08:08.020
<v Ira Glass>They've told the story so many times that at this point they're not exactly sure they even have the facts right. Which means something to them, because they are reporters. Facts are their business. And, in fact, this story began as an assignment in journalism school. Alex turned in the story for a class, but, even after he did that, the two of them continued to run down leads and interview people for the story. They could not stop themselves, such is their crush on this story. The story begins in Central Park in New York City at some party thrown by some big company.
<v Ira Glass>他们把这个故事讲了太多次，以至于现在他们甚至不确定自己讲的事实是否准确。这让他们很在意，因为他们是记者。事实是他们的本职工作。事实上，这个故事最初是新闻学院的一个作业。亚历克斯为一门课交了这个故事，但在那之后，他们两人还是继续追寻线索并采访相关人员。他们无法停止，因为他们对这个故事太痴迷了。故事开始于纽约中央公园的一场大型公司聚会。

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:17.910
<v Alex Lane>Somehow, a young wannabe actor, aspiring actor of Puerto Rican descent from Brooklyn, named Johnny de Villa winds up at this party.
<v Alex Lane>不知怎的，一位来自布鲁克林的、波多黎各血统的年轻、渴望表演的演员，名叫约翰尼·德·维拉，闯进了这个聚会。

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:19.430
<v Ira Glass>You mean, just like wanders in?
<v Ira Glass>你是说，就像溜进去那样？

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:24.630
<v Alex  Lane>Yeah, maybe he's there with a girlfriend. I'm sure Eric recalls why he's there.
<v Alex  Lane>是的，也许他是和女朋友一起去的。我相信埃里克记得他为什么在那儿。

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:35.630
<v Eric Holm>I have no recollection whatsoever. But I think sometime we say, he crashed the party. And sometimes we say that he-- in any case, he got there early, very early, and he stayed late, very late.
<v Eric Holm>我完全没印象了。但我认为有时我们会说，他硬闯进来的。有时我们又说他——总之，他去得很早，非常早，走得很晚，非常晚。

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:37.620
<v Alex Lane>And he drank.
<v Alex Lane>而且他喝了酒。

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:41.590
<v Eric Holm>And it may well be, as we recall it now, that he smoked a lot of pot.
<v Eric Holm>照我们现在的回忆，很可能他抽了不少大麻。

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:42.830
<v Alex Lane>Smoked a marijuana cigarette.
<v Alex Lane>抽了一支大麻烟。

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:45.010
<v Ira  Glass>But it may not be.
<v Ira  Glass>但也许没有。

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:45.930
<v Alex Lane>It may not be.
<v Alex Lane>也许没有。

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:47.150
<v Eric Holm>That's the way we tell it now.
<v Eric Holm>这就是我们现在的说法。

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:48.400
<v Ira Glass>That he's high?
<v Ira Glass>他吸嗨了吗？

00:08:48.400 --> 00:09:01.450
<v Eric Holm>Yeah, but in any case he decides to wander out on his own at about 1:30 or 2:00 in the morning. And he wanders through the park for a little while. And he wanders through these gates that are open.
<v Eric Holm>是的，但无论如何，他在凌晨1点半或2点左右决定独自溜出去。他在公园里游荡了一会儿。然后他穿过了那些敞开的大门。

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:16.230
<v Alex Lane>Well, yeah. In this section of the story, we should say is his recollection, what he later told police. So he wanders in and, before he knows it, there's sort of a distant splashing and sea lions. And he's just sort of walking and thinking.
<v Alex Lane>嗯，是的。这一部分故事应该说是他的回忆，是他后来告诉警察的。所以他走进去，还没回过神，就听到远处有水花声和海狮的声音。他只是走着，想着事情。

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:21.670
<v Ira Glass>And he thinks that literally, they accidentally just left the gates open to the entire zoo. That's his story?
<v Ira Glass>他真的认为他们不小心把整个动物园的大门都敞开了？这是他的故事？

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:24.850
<v Eric Holm>And, of course, the zoo told us that that would be absolutely impossible.
<v Eric Holm>当然，动物园告诉我们那绝对是不可能的。

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:28.980
<v Alex Lane>Maybe there's some plausibility, but it's unlikely that they left the aviary open.
<v Alex Lane>也许有一定的合理性，但他们不太可能把鸟舍留着没关。

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:40.100
<v Eric Holm>Which is where Johnny found himself after a little bit of wandering. And the door is wide open. He strolls right in, and, the way he tells it, there was a long line of cages.
<v Eric Holm>约翰尼溜达了一会儿后发现自己就在那儿。门大开着。他直接走了进去，据他所说，里面有一长排笼子。

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:40.940
<v Alex Lane>There was a mist.
<v Alex Lane>有一层雾。

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:42.140
<v Eric Holm>A mist, he says.
<v Eric Holm>一层雾，他说。

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:44.390
<v Ira Glass>A mist indoors?
<v Ira Glass>室内有雾？

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:46.830
<v Eric Holm>Well, he may have been high.
<v Eric Holm>嗯，他可能吸嗨了。

00:09:46.830 --> 00:10:06.850
<v Alex Lane>Or he may not have. He definitely said, there was this mist. And the mist sort of cleared. and before him was a parrot with brilliant green plumage and a red tuft on its head. And they locked eyes.
<v Alex Lane>或者他可能没有。他确实说过，有一层雾。雾散开后，在他面前是一只鹦鹉，有着明亮的绿色羽毛和红色的头冠。他们对视了。

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:11.700
<v Eric Holm>He looked at the parrot. The parrot looked at him. And they had an instant connection, he says.
<v Eric Holm>他看着鹦鹉。鹦鹉看着他。他说，他们产生了瞬间的共鸣。

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:24.910
<v Alex Lane>And so he says to the parrot, "you want to go?" And the parrot says to him, "yeah." And he says, "Let's go."
<v Alex Lane>于是他对鹦鹉说：“你想走吗？”鹦鹉对他说：“想。”于是他说：“那我们走吧。”

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:31.510
<v Ira Glass>The parrot says him, "yeah?"
<v Ira Glass>鹦鹉对他说“想”？

00:10:31.510 --> 00:10:32.780
<v Eric Holm>As he recalls.
<v Eric Holm>他是这么回忆的。

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:36.360
<v Alex Lane>As he recalls. Which is unlikely, because this is not a talking parrot.
